-### The ledger
-
-The ledger sits beside this guide. [`ledger/departures.md`](ledger/departures.md) records every
-declaration the built cascade writes differently from Bootstrap 5.3.8, and
-[`ledger/additions.md`](ledger/additions.md) records every name this cascade emits that the official
-inventory lacks. `readDepartures` and `readAdditions` in `tests/setupServer.ts` read those files, and
-the `cascade ledger` gates in `tests/conformance.test.ts` compare each one against what
-`collectLedger` measures, so a difference no row records and a row no difference carries redden the
-conformance proof. Refresh each file with `npm run build:src && npm run test:conformance`.
+### Departures
+
+Each row names one declaration the built cascade writes differently from Bootstrap 5.3.8, keyed the
+way the official inventory is keyed: the component, the selector, the property, and the at-rule
+enclosing the declaration. One table follows per component, in the order the shipped keys sort.
+`readDepartures` in `tests/setupServer.ts` reads every one of them, and `collectLedger` measures the
+same comparison over the unminified compile, so the conformance proof reddens on a difference no row
+records and on a row no difference carries. Refresh the rows with
+`npm run build:src && npm run test:conformance`: the ledger gates print every row to add and every
+row to strike, one per line, as that row's cells joined by `|` in column order.
+
+The comparison is textual. It reads the value each side writes, never the value a browser computes,
+so a row can record a difference in notation whose computed value matches: `transparent` against
+`rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` and `87.5%` against `0.875em` are such rows.
+
+A `—` in `Condition` records a declaration under no at-rule, and a `—` in either value column
+records a side that writes no declaration there at all. `(empty)` records a side that writes the
+declaration with nothing after the colon, which leaves the property without a value while the
+declaration itself stands. The `Departure` cell names why the two values differ: `tokenized` routes
+the release value through a Veneer token, `aliased` reads another compatibility variable in its
+place, `fallback` keeps the release value behind a `var()` fallback, `dropped` writes no declaration
+at all, and `declared` writes the value in a form or at a value the other members do not name. An
+empty value against a recorded one is `declared` rather than `dropped`, because the cascade writes
+the declaration.
+
